#
# procd reads this file once ubusd runs. The format is
# <id>:<runlevels>:<action>:<process>: the id names the terminal (empty: none,
# the program reads and writes /dev/null), the runlevels are ignored.
# procd starts the "respawn" programs first, then runs the boot script, and
# once it is done the "respawnlate" ones; a program of either kind that ends
# is started again half a second later. procd reads up to 127 characters of a
# line and the first 7 words of a command: a longer one is cut short.

# The system log, in memory: syslogd keeps /run/messages and one older copy
# (its default), of 64 KiB each, and klogd copies the kernel's messages into it.
::respawn:/sbin/syslogd -n -O /run/messages -s 64
::respawn:/sbin/klogd -n

# netifd brings up the interfaces that its configuration in /run/config names;
# the boot script and the stick's scripts write it (onu-net).
::respawn:/usr/sbin/netifd -c /run/config -r /run/resolv.conf.auto

# The boot script, /etc/init.d/rcS, as /etc/rc.d/S10boot: procd runs the
# scripts of /etc/rc.d whose names begin with S, in order, with "boot".
::sysinit:/etc/init.d/rcS S boot

# The ssh server and the status daemon, on the host side's addresses, which
# the boot script brought up (onu-run).
::respawnlate:/usr/sbin/onu-run ssh
::respawnlate:/usr/sbin/onu-run pond

# A login prompt on the console, started again whenever the session ends. The
# one account is root, with the password that ssh and the panel take too
# (onu-store keeps it; until one is set it is "admin"). A bench may have root's
# shell there without the password (onu-run, CONSOLE_AUTOLOGIN in
# /etc/board.conf). The speed stays the one the boot loader set, 115200 baud.
console::respawnlate:/usr/sbin/onu-run console
